{"decision":{"at":"2026-10-01T15:33:08-05:00","what":"The gear and 'More' (⋯) buttons the owner meant are not in Widget.js at all — Widget.js just calls ext/Chat/Composer.js's composer(), which builds both buttons (⋯ toggled a small popover with Dictate's engine/status info; the gear toggled Mic.js's settings_panel()). Merged them in Composer.js/Mic.js/Chat.css instead: ONE ⚙ button now opens ONE full-screen panel (position:fixed, inset:0 — same shape as ext/Chat/Drill.js's .chatbox-drill) holding both the engine/status info block and the settings grid. This fixes every surface that uses composer() at once (ChatPanel, AI2, the Dictate Widget) — CLAUDE.md law 6 — instead of forking a second copy inside Widget.js.","alternative":"could have faked a merge inside Widget.js alone, but the real buttons live in Composer.js/Mic.js, so that would not have actually merged anything for any surface. Also updated ext/Chat/readme.md and ux/Dictate/readme.md so the docs describe the new one-button full-screen menu instead of the old two popovers."}}
{"log":{"at":"2026-10-01T15:34:00-05:00","msg":"BLOCKED on verification and landing: this CLI session has no granted tool permissions beyond Read/Glob/Grep/Edit/Write and a tiny Bash allowlist (whoami, date, git read-only, `node --version` only). Every call below answered 'Claude requested permissions ... but you haven't granted it yet' or 'This command requires approval', with no prompt reachable in this non-interactive session: mcp__servex__take_worktree, mcp__servex__list_cards, mcp__site__pages, mcp__site__shot (the screenshot the brief asks for). Bash itself refused `node --check <file>`, `node .claude/hooks/append.mjs ...`, `node Server/hold.mjs status`, and `node Server/merge.mjs` the same way (both in the Bash tool and the PowerShell tool) — only `node --version` is allowed. So: no node --check on the two edited .js files, no hold.mjs around the direct-main-tree writes, no screenshot proof, no merge.mjs smoke test, no finish-task (needs the documentation + review gate, both of which need these same tools). This matches the minion skill's own warning almost exactly: 'a minion launched without --permission-mode bypassPermissions reads the repo and writes nothing' — except here Edit/Write DO work, just not Bash execution or any MCP tool. I verified the two .js files and the .css file by eye instead — read the full file before and after every edit, counted braces, checked for stray `*/` and unbalanced quotes, and reviewed the exact `git diff` of all three (git itself is NOT blocked) — but that is not the same guarantee node --check gives, and nobody has loaded the page to see it render. This needs a session with real tool grants (or the owner/mastermind picking it up) to: run `node --check` on Composer.js and Mic.js, load /framework/ext/Chat/panel/ or /framework/ux/Dictate/ and click the ⚙ to prove the full-screen menu opens/closes and both halves (info + settings) show, then run node Server/merge.mjs and finish-task. The code change itself is done and described in the 'decision' line above; only verification and landing are blocked."}}

{"assign":{"session_id":"4f16fa02-4fe3-435f-ba10-892419023482","agent":"minion-card-threaded-replies","card":"card-threaded-replies","brief":"Owner, on /framework/ux/Dictate/ (2026-10-01): 'it doesn't seem like we're responding to specific cards yet, like in a threaded way. I think that would be a useful thing to do.' Context: ai2/card.js cards already have a related pattern — ux/Understand's ClarifyCard (a per-sentence clarification card you tap into, answer, which resolves that one specific item, see ux/Understand/Understand.js). The owner wants the SAME kind of specific-card threading for `card_reply`/the card chat generally: a reply that targets one particular card (or sub-item within a card) rather than only ever appending to the card's flat top-level chat. Read public/framework/ai2/card.js, card_reply's mechanics (Servex MCP tool + its backing store), and ux/Understand/Understand.js's ClarifyCard pattern for precedent. Scope the smallest version that lets a reply be clearly tied to (and visually nested under) one card/sub-card, implement it, verify on the live AI board, and land per finish-task.","model":"claude-sonnet-5","requested_at":"2026-10-01T15:15:35-05:00","now":"starting","steps":[],"step":1}}
